    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to study for my signals class and im totally stuck on the fourier transform. i get the math formulas but i dont really get the actual intuition. like, what is it actually doing to a signal? can u explain it simply?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Think of the Fourier Transform as a sophisticated musical analyzer for signals. Instead of seeing a signal over time (how loud it is at any given moment), the transform tells you *what* frequencies are present in that signal and *how strong* each one is. It essentially breaks down any complex wave into its fundamental sine and cosine waves. This allows engineers to see the spectral \"ingredients\" of a signal.",
